  "text": "To determine the number of valid passwords, we need to calculate the total number of possible 5-character passwords and then subtract the number of passwords that contain a consecutive sequence of 3 or more characters that appears in the company's name, \"StartTime\".\n\n1. Calculate the total number of possible passwords:\n\nSince each character can be a letter (A-Z) or a digit (0-9), there are 26 letters + 10 digits = 36 possible characters for each position in the password.\n\nThe total number of possible 5-character passwords is:\n\\[ 36^5 = 60,466,176 \\]\n\n2. Identify and count the number of passwords that contain a consecutive sequence of 3 or more characters that appears in \"StartTime\":\n\nWe will look for sequences that appear in \"StartTime\", which are [ST], [ST1], [ST12], [ST123]. These are the only sequences that can appear in the password without forming a longer sequence.\n\n- ST: There are two occurrences of \"ST\" in \"StartTime\"\n  - ST followed by any 3 characters (4 possible sequences)\n- ST1: There is one occurrence of \"ST1\" in \"StartTime\"\n  - ST1 followed by any 2 characters (30 possible sequences)\n- ST12: There is one occurrence of \"ST12\" in \"StartTime\"\n  - ST12 followed by any 1 character (36 possible sequences)\n- ST123: There is one occurrence of \"ST123\" in \"StartTime\"\n  - ST123 (only one possible sequence)\n\nCombining all possibilities, we get:\n\\[ 2 \\times 4 + 1 \\times 30 + 1 \\times 36 + 1 \\times 1 = 8 + 30 + 36 + 1 = 75 \\]\n\nEach of these sequences can appear in all positions of the password, so the number of complete passwords that violate the rule is 75.\n\n3. Subtract the invalid passwords from the total:\n\n\\[ \\text{Valid Passwords} = 60,466,176 - 75 = 60,466,001 \\]\n\nThus, the number of valid passwords is \\(\\boxed{60466001}\\).",
